WEBVTT

1
00:00:01.040 --> 00:00:05.460
Primary reason that I chose to go to LSU was because I wanted to be governor of Louisiana.

2
00:00:05.460 --> 00:00:14.400
But the second reason, and it was a close second, was because [laughing] . . . When we moved to Lafayette, my parents always enjoyed athletics,

3
00:00:14.400 --> 00:00:17.920
as did I, and the best athletics in the state of Louisiana—you have to realize,

4
00:00:17.920 --> 00:00:21.920
there were no [New Orleans] Saints or anything like that back then—was LSU athletics.

5
00:00:21.920 --> 00:00:23.500
Even though USL [University of Southwestern Louisiana, now the University of Louisiana at Lafayette] had a football team,

6
00:00:23.500 --> 00:00:26.840
they were kind of minor league and LSU was big stuff.

7
00:00:26.840 --> 00:00:32.960
So we got . . . My dad got, even though we had no connection as a family whatsoever to LSU,

8
00:00:32.960 --> 00:00:37.660
my father got season tickets . . . football season tickets in Tiger Stadium.

9
00:00:37.660 --> 00:00:44.380
And we started going to the games from the time I was . . . we started . . . We won the National Championship in 1958.

10
00:00:44.380 --> 00:00:50.900
We started going in ’55 or ’56.

11
00:00:50.900 --> 00:00:53.360
So I was like nine or ten years old.

12
00:00:53.360 --> 00:00:56.680
And Paul Dietzel was hired then.

13
00:00:56.680 --> 00:01:03.300
We went through some miserable seasons, and then in 1958 we win the National Championship.

14
00:01:03.300 --> 00:01:09.840
And 1959 was even more exciting because that was the Billy Cannon Halloween run against Ole Miss.

15
00:01:09.840 --> 00:01:12.180
And I was there with my dad and my mom.

16
00:01:12.180 --> 00:01:17.080
I mean, that was the most unbelievable sight!

17
00:01:17.080 --> 00:01:26.600
You never saw sixty-seven thousand people—except for the few thousand that were Ole Miss fans—act as one.

18
00:01:26.600 --> 00:01:32.100
And you know, I mean, for one fleeting moment that whole stadium was one.

19
00:01:32.100 --> 00:01:36.920
I mean you could have gotten any reform in Louisiana done, you know, in that one moment.

20
00:01:36.920 --> 00:01:49.040
I mean, the poor Cajun mechanic from Napoleonville [Louisiana] was hugging the banker from Homer, Louisiana, in north Louisiana.

21
00:01:49.040 --> 00:01:52.140
I mean, people were just . . . I mean, it was unbelievable!

22
00:01:52.140 --> 00:01:54.460
And everybody was crying.

23
00:01:54.460 --> 00:01:56.740
I mean it was just unreal.

24
00:01:56.740 --> 00:01:59.060
So, Billy Cannon became a major hero.

25
00:01:59.060 --> 00:02:06.260
Well . . . And was before that night, from the time he was like a freshman or sophomore, I had big scrapbooks and everything.

26
00:02:06.260 --> 00:02:15.520
We went on a field trip up to Baton Rouge; I think it was our freshman or sophomore year in high school.

27
00:02:15.520 --> 00:02:16.880
I can’t remember when it was.

28
00:02:16.880 --> 00:02:23.220
But we went up to the [State] Capitol [Building] and did all the stuff, and I had asked that we go out to LSU.

29
00:02:23.220 --> 00:02:34.000
We did, and they let us go into Tiger Stadium and damned if Billy Cannon wasn’t coming either to or from practice and stopped with a number of us.

30
00:02:34.000 --> 00:02:35.320
Of course all of the kids were saying, “Oh,

31
00:02:35.320 --> 00:02:40.760
Roger is just going bananas!” you know and . . . And I had my . . . still have somewhere in the scrapbook,

32
00:02:40.760 --> 00:02:46.120
a little Instamatic photograph that one of my classmates took of me and Billy Cannon.

33
00:02:46.120 --> 00:02:48.440
Billy Cannon up here and me down here.

34
00:02:48.440 --> 00:02:53.800
He was an All-American for like three out of his four years and I think this was like his sophomore year.

35
00:02:53.800 --> 00:03:00.340
So he was my great hero and LSU football was my love of, you know, of hero worship kind of stuff.
